 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Ah, yes.  Modern management training.  The new stomping grounds of motivational speakers.  Motivational speakers found that it was too hard to get the public to pay exhorbitant fees to come to their seminars and learn silly gimicks to make them more motivated, so they went after upper management.  So now you get these two-day "retreats" that managers go on, and when they come back, they're all "fired up" to use the new catch-phrases, gimicks, and other parlor tricks to try to keep people motivated.  We had one manager make up booklets for all of our team.  And each team member was supposed to write compliments in every other team member's book.  Did you know that I was smart and creative?  Times 10?  And, as it turns out, all of my coworkers are, too.  <br>
<br>
Even managers who don't go to the brainwashing seminars buy into it, because "it's the way things are done."  They pick up the buzz-words, because, well, why would you use one phrase to describe something when everyone else is using a much more "catchy" phrase?<br>
<br>
That's why I really liked my last boss.  He would joke around with us, he would take us out for drinks, he would let us screw around as long as our work got done and as long as the client didn't know.  But he was also a very good manager, and we always remembered that, and had alot of respect for him professionally.  <br>
<br>
I respect managers more when they treat me like a person, rather than a "human resource".  I'm much more willing to take the crap jobs that they sometimes have to hand down, and I'm much more willing to put forth a "proactive" effort when I'm helping out someone I like, rather than just someone I work for.  If it's just a job, I do the work I'm paid for and that's it.  When I enjoy it, I'm much more likely to put more into it.  I just wish more managers understood this.<br>
<br>
__________________<br>
If I were creating a world, I wouldn't mess about with butterflies and daffodils! I would've started with lasers, eight o'clock, day one!</font></p>

